    {
      "id": "grammar-evaluative-adjective-before-noun",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "adjetivo de valoración + sustantivo",
      "questionEn": "Where do common judgment words such as bueno or malo often go?",
      "answerEn": "Put them before the noun when they give an overall evaluation: buena idea, mala noticia.",
      "noteEn": "Spanish often puts a neutral descriptive word after its noun, but common evaluation words can come before it. The describing word still changes to match the noun: bueno becomes buena before a feminine singular noun.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Fue una buena decisión.",
        "en": "It was a good decision."
      },
      "tags": [
